{"duration_api_ms":143949,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"eda69b0d-7738-4988-b328-522079921e33","total_cost_usd":0.4594958,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10182,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":18887,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9165},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10182,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18887,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10182,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10182},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":18887,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10182,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4594958,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":9165,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\\n\\nEvery credential has its own cap. When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge, without waking the service behind it, so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal.\\n\\nRequests are counted against the credential that presented them, never against a connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway does not disclose. Opening more sockets therefore buys a caller nothing: the count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts. A client spread across many worker processes, eight say, is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been, and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\\n\\nThe default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which covers every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen; a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential. A credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window. A refusal is not an outage and not a bug in the gateway, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it. It is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer. Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\\n\\nAn earlier draft of this guidance argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway’s own counters, and that approach is deliberately left out: two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the Retry-After header exists.\\n\\n## How to read a refusal\\n\\nEvery refusal carries a Retry-After header, and its value is a whole number of seconds to wait. Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract: a client that does exactly that needs nothing else from this document, which is the outcome it was written for, however unlikely that makes it to be read at all. The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller nothing.\\n\\nThe body of the refusal is JSON, and it repeats as plain fields the cap, the tier and what the gateway calls the “window” it counted in. None of those fields is a substitute for the header: a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice, and has written code whose only possible future is to disagree with the gateway it is talking to. The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused; nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\\n\\n## How to retry well\\n\\nThere are four rules, and the order they are given in is the order to apply them.\\n\\n1. Honour the header, every time, and do not compute your own wait. The gateway has already done that arithmetic with information the caller cannot see, and the same rule holds on the 503 path, even though the cause of that refusal is entirely different. A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see, and there is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\\n\\n2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that are not a success. A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path: the platform itself is overloaded. The four cases are not interchangeable. A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive. Avoid writing that loop.\\n\\n3. Batch is different. A batch credential’s allowance is measured over exactly the same window as the default, not under a separate counting scheme. The tier is set on the credential when it is issued and cannot be asked for per call. The window, the header and the body are identical to the interactive case, so a client written for one tier needs no change at all to run against the other. Nothing else about the two tiers differs.\\n\\n4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week. The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait. A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf. The counts are the whole of the argument, and without them a request is only a preference.\\n\\n## Other refusals and their codes\\n\\nNot all refusals are caps. The gateway refuses a request for three reasons, and only one of them is the cap described above; the other two have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in. A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit, in which case it is refused before it has been read at all. None of those three clears itself by waiting for a stated number of seconds.\\n\\nThe distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader. A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and the log afterwards shows nothing but a long run of refusals and no successes, which reads like an outage and is not one; the wasted budget is the caller’s own. Reading the status code rather than the class it belongs to is what separates the two cases, and it costs one comparison.\\n\\n## Asking for an increase\\n\\nA cap can be raised, but not on the strength of an assertion that the current one is too small: the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0. Bring the refusal counts for a full week, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving. The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all; a burst is cheaper to smooth out than it is to serve at its peak.\\n\\nA caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all. That is the cheapest fix available to anybody, it is available to almost everybody, and it is the outcome everyone prefers.\\n\\nRequests reach the platform team through the usual channel and are answered within two working days. There is no expedited path and no exception list, and chasing an answer does not make it arrive any sooner.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# What a client should do when the gateway says 429\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\"reason\":\"Base document's title, which names the subject completely.\"},{\"slot\":\"batch allowance relative to default\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"allowed 1000 in the same window (default 100)\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"fifty times the default allowance\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"1000 requests in a window, which is 50 times the default\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"allowed 1000 in the same window (default 100)\",\"reason\":\"1000 against a default of 100 is not 50 times; the explicit values are kept.\"},{\"slot\":\"heading for retry rules\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"## How to retry well\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"## What the client does\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"## How to retry well\",\"reason\":\"Base heading for the same section.\"},{\"slot\":\"heading for non-cap refusals\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"## Other refusals and their codes\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"## Other kinds of refusal\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"## Other refusals and their codes\",\"reason\":\"Base heading for the same section.\"},{\"slot\":\"placement of cap-increase content\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"## Asking for an increase\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"under ## Other kinds of refusal\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"## Asking for an increase\",\"reason\":\"Base has a dedicated section for requesting a larger cap.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\"reason\":\"Base title kept.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Every credential has its own cap.\",\"reason\":\"Opening line takes b2's 'own' phrasing.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Every credential has its own cap.\",\"reason\":\"Same opening fact, stated once.\"},{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge, without waking the service behind it, so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal.\",\"reason\":\"Dashes replaced with commas.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge, without waking the service behind it, so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal.\",\"reason\":\"Same edge-refusal fact as a3.\"},{\"segment\":\"b4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"An earlier draft of this guidance argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway’s own counters, and that approach is deliberately left out: two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the Retry-After header exists.\",\"reason\":\"Token-bucket rationale kept, header named.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, never against a connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway does not disclose.\",\"reason\":\"Counting rule merged with b5's undisclosed window start.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, never against a connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway does not disclose.\",\"reason\":\"Undisclosed start added to a4's counting rule.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Opening more sockets therefore buys a caller nothing: the count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Sockets point joined with b7's across-hosts detail.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"Opening more sockets therefore buys a caller nothing: the count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Same sockets point as a5.\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Opening more sockets therefore buys a caller nothing: the count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts.\",\"reason\":\"Across-hosts detail carried in the sockets sentence.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A client spread across many worker processes, eight say, is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been, and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\",\"reason\":\"Eight-process example combined with b8's general case.\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"A client spread across many worker processes, eight say, is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been, and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\",\"reason\":\"General case combined with a6's eight-process example.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which covers every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen; a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential. A credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window.\",\"reason\":\"Allowances split around b9's interactive-use note.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which covers every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen; a caller that needs more is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential.\",\"reason\":\"Interactive-use note attached to the default allowance.\"},{\"segment\":\"a8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A refusal is not an outage and not a bug in the gateway, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it.\",\"reason\":\"b10's not-a-bug point added.\"},{\"segment\":\"b10\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"A refusal is not an outage and not a bug in the gateway, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it. It is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer.\",\"reason\":\"Carried by a8 and a9 together.\"},{\"segment\":\"b11\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\",\"reason\":\"Same point as a10.\"},{\"segment\":\"b12\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"## How to read a refusal\",\"reason\":\"Identical heading.\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Every refusal carries a Retry-After header, and its value is a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\"reason\":\"Header fact merged with its value and b14's every-refusal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a13\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Every refusal carries a Retry-After header, and its value is a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\"reason\":\"Value merged into header sentence.\"},{\"segment\":\"b13\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"Every refusal carries a Retry-After header, and its value is a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\"reason\":\"Header name already carried.\"},{\"segment\":\"b14\",\"disposition\":\"subsumed\",\"replacement\":\"Every refusal carries a Retry-After header,